      "text": "```json\n{\"unit_id\": \"Minemaes\", \"choices\": [], \"reasoning\": \"C1 Mnw-ms.w (Min-mesu) is the god Min plus ms.w, 'born (of)', from msj. Its consonants m-n-m-s can be found in Minemaes once w is left unwritten, but consonants alone do not decide the match. Read as a Dodson & Hilton modern spelling, Minemaes does not render Min plus ms. Their usual rendering of ms is -mose (Minmose, Ahmose) or -mes/-messu. In their spellings, 'em' after a god's name renders the preposition m (Amenemhat, Amenemopet). A final '-aes' renders a word with ꜥ or ꜣ plus s, often the suffix =s, as in Senebhenaes for ḥnꜥ=s. So Minemaes reads as Min + m + a further word (ꜥ/ꜣ + s), not as Min-mesu. These are different words, so C1 is a different name. Review X counted consonants only and ignored what 'em' and 'ae' render. C2 Jmn-ms.w (Amun-mesu) is the god Amun plus ms.w. Record 1 renders Min, not Amun, and its later part does not render ms.w either, so C2 is not the same name.\"}\n```"
